# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated files: written by a program, never versioned
#
# Two sets, from three writers. matugen renders the first from the current
# wallpaper, so they change every time the background does; `desktop-opacity`
# and `desktop-font` write the second from the settings window. What is
# versioned is the template or the config that READS them, never the result.
#
# THESE RULES CANNOT MATCH ANYTHING TODAY, and that is not a reason to drop
# them. install.sh stows with --no-folding, which creates real directories and
# links file by file, so a program writing a new file into ~/.config lands in
# ~/.config and not in this repository -- verified: none of the paths below
# exists here, while every one of them exists under ~. The rules are the net
# for the day somebody stows WITHOUT --no-folding, where the whole directory
# is linked and every one of these would appear as an untracked file inside
# the repo. An earlier version of this comment said that showing up as
# "modified" after a stow was expected; with --no-folding it is not, and if it
# ever happens something is wrong rather than normal.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# From the wallpaper, by matugen.
kitty/.config/kitty/colors.conf
hypr/.config/hypr/colors.lua
niri/.config/niri/colors.kdl
gtk/.config/gtk-3.0/gtk.css
gtk/.config/gtk-4.0/gtk.css
ranger/.config/ranger/accent
quickshell/.config/quickshell/colors.json

# From the settings window, by desktop-opacity and desktop-font. The configs
# that include them -- kitty.conf and userChrome.css -- are versioned; these
# are not.
kitty/.config/kitty/opacity.conf
kitty/.config/kitty/font.conf

# Zen writes outside .config: its chrome/ only counts inside the profile,
# ~/.zen/rice. The versioned part is userChrome.css (zen/.zen/rice/chrome/),
# which imports both of these.
zen/.zen/rice/chrome/colors.css
zen/.zen/rice/chrome/opacity.css

# zathura and fastfetch are generated WHOLE from a template: they have no stow
# package of their own. See matugen/.config/matugen/config.toml.
#
# ~/.config/qt6ct/colors/matugen.conf is generated too, but there is nothing to
# ignore: qt6ct has no stow package either. Its config is a seed (seeds/), so
# the whole directory is the machine's and matugen writes outside the repo.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime state
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
openrgb/.config/OpenRGB/logs/
openrgb/.config/OpenRGB/plugins/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Executables that live in bin/ but are not this repo's to ship
#
# Two different cases, both landing in ~/.local/bin and neither belonging in
# git. cship is COMPILED: its configuration is versioned under shell/ and the
# binary is built, not stored. claude is INSTALLED BY ITS OWN TOOL -- what is
# at that path is a symlink into ~/.local/share/claude/versions/, so tracking
# it would version a pointer at one machine's install. Its icon
# (icons/.local/share/icons/claude.png) IS tracked, because that one really is
# an asset the desktop entry needs and the application does not provide.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
bin/.local/bin/cship
bin/.local/bin/claude

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# General junk
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.bak
*.old
*~
.cache/

# Links created by `systemctl --user enable`. They are regenerated, not
# versioned: install.sh does it.
systemd/.config/systemd/user/*.target.wants/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Machine-local Hyprland pieces
#
# monitors.lua and tweaks.lua are written by the `desktop-monitors` and
# `desktop-tweak` scripts from the settings window; local.lua is hand-written and
# holds whatever belongs to one machine or one person (keyboard layout, extra
# binds, window rules). None of them may be versioned: the repo is shared with
# another machine and another layout, and a tracked personal file turns every
# pull over there into a conflict. hyprland.lua loads all three with pcall, so
# not having them is the normal case.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
hypr/.config/hypr/monitors.lua
hypr/.config/hypr/tweaks.lua
hypr/.config/hypr/local.lua

# Generated by `wallpaper-interval` from the settings window: a systemd
# drop-in overriding the tracked unit's interval. Machine-local, like
# monitors.lua and tweaks.lua.
systemd/.config/systemd/user/wallpaper-rotate.timer.d/

# The niri flavor's machine-local pieces, and they are four different things.
#
# tweaks.kdl is generated by `desktop-tweak`, the same script and the same state
# file that write tweaks.lua above -- gaps, corner radius, focus-ring width,
# pointer, key repeat and keyboard layouts. It is an override layer applied on
# top of config.kdl's own literals, exactly like its Hyprland counterpart.
#
# monitors.kdl is generated by `desktop-monitors` from the settings window, the
# way monitors.lua is on the Hyprland side -- with one difference worth knowing
# before touching either: there it is an override layer applied ON TOP of the
# hand-written block, and here it is the ONLY declaration of any output.
# config.kdl declares none, because an `output` block in an include is ignored
# when the including file names the same monitor, so layering is not available.
#
# main-monitor.kdl comes out of the same script and the same choice as
# monitors.kdl, and is a SECOND file rather than more of that one because the
# two want opposite positions in config.kdl: an output block has to be included
# before anything else names a monitor, and this holds window rules, which have
# to be included after the rules they override. It is why config.kdl's window
# rules no longer name a monitor at all -- one machine's EDID string used to sit
# there five times over, and on the other machine those five rules matched
# nothing without ever saying so.
#
# local.kdl is hand-written and holds whatever belongs to one machine or one
# person (keyboard layout, extra binds, window rules). None of the four may be
# versioned: the repo is shared with another machine and another layout, and a
# tracked personal file turns every pull over there into a conflict. All four
# are included with optional=true, so not having them is the normal case.
niri/.config/niri/tweaks.kdl
niri/.config/niri/monitors.kdl
niri/.config/niri/main-monitor.kdl
niri/.config/niri/local.kdl

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The backup's per-machine half, and the only entry in this file that is about
# a SECRET rather than about a generated file
#
# THIS REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. `gh repo view --json visibility` says PUBLIC, and
# a passphrase pushed to it is not fixed by a later commit removing it -- it is
# in the history, on GitHub's servers, and in every clone and fork made in the
# meantime. The only version of that mistake that can be undone is the one that
# never happens.
#
# So the split is the same one monitors.kdl and tweaks.lua already use, applied
# to something that matters more. backup/.config/borgmatic/config.yaml is
# tracked and holds what to save, what to leave out and for how long. local.env
# holds this machine's two values -- BORG_REPO, where the archives go, and
# BORG_PASSPHRASE, what opens them -- and is never committed.
#
# LIKE THE RULES AT THE TOP OF THIS FILE, IT CANNOT MATCH ANYTHING TODAY.
# install.sh stows with --no-folding, so a file written into
# ~/.config/borgmatic/ lands there and not in this repository. The rule is the
# net for the day somebody stows without it, and for the day somebody creates
# the file inside the clone by hand because that is where the rest of the
# configuration lives. The glob is second on purpose: an .env under this
# package is a machine's own environment by definition, whatever it ends up
# being called.
#
# tests/backup-secrets.sh is the other half of the same guard: this file stops
# the untracked half being added, that check stops a literal being pasted into
# the tracked half. Both are needed -- neither catches the other's mistake.
backup/.config/borgmatic/local.env
backup/.config/borgmatic/*.env
